1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,500
Right now I'm standing at the counter of our neighborhood pharmacy, fake-coughing.

2
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:05,000
"*cough, cough*"

3
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:08,333
Even though it's my kid who has the cold.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:13,000
Let's rewind one minute.

5
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:17,633
My kid caught a cold, so we went to the pediatrician down the street.

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:22,233
While we waited for the medicine at the pharmacy downstairs, my kid tugged at my sleeve.

7
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,900
"Dad, what's the difference between a doctor, a pharmacist and a nurse?"

8
00:00:27,967 --> 00:00:29,233
"They all wear white."

9
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,733
I answered without a second thought.

10
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,433
"The doctor's the boss, the nurse is the helper,"

11
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,067
"and the pharmacist is... the person who sells medicine?"

12
00:00:39,133 --> 00:00:41,933
The pharmacy went quiet.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,467
The pharmacist, holding a bag of medicine, had frozen mid-motion.

14
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:48,833
"Uh... I mean... *cough, cough*"

15
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:54,100
My kid had the cold. I had the cough.

16
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:56,267
"I'll call you when the medicine's ready."

17
00:00:56,333 --> 00:01:01,433
I'd lined them up like a ladder, top to bottom. Looking back, the ladder itself was wrong.

18
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,767
I sat down on the waiting bench and quietly opened my phone.

19
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:10,033
The father, when cornered, will search the internet even in a pharmacy.

20
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:14,600
No wonder I mixed them up. All three wear white, all three work around hospitals,

21
00:01:14,667 --> 00:01:16,633
and we call all three of them 'teacher'.

22
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,100
And the person who took my kid's temperature at the clinic earlier?

23
00:01:20,167 --> 00:01:24,667
I'd assumed she was a nurse. Her name badge said 'nursing assistant'.

24
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:31,667
The names are similar, but the paths are different. A nurse studies nursing at university and passes a national exam for a license,

25
00:01:31,733 --> 00:01:36,300
while a nursing assistant finishes a set training course and takes a separate national exam for a certificate.

26
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:44,967
Nursing assistants help with care and treatment under a nurse or doctor. At small neighborhood clinics, they're often the ones we meet.

27
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:51,367
With so many people in white, we picture a ladder: the doctor on top, everyone else below.

28
00:01:51,433 --> 00:01:57,433
But they're not above or below each other. They're more like different seats around one sick person.

29
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:02,233
So today we'll look at just one thing: around one sick person, who does what.

30
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:08,867
First, the doctor. That day he looked at my kid's throat, listened to the chest, asked a few questions,

31
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:11,767
and decided: 'It's a cold.'

32
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:17,133
Working out what's wrong and why, then writing the right medicine on a prescription.

33
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:21,733
But the doctor didn't hand us any medicine. Just a piece of paper.

34
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:30,367
Here's the first split. Around 1240, the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick the Second made an unusual law.

35
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:36,000
Doctors couldn't also run pharmacies, and medicine had to be sold at set prices.

36
00:02:36,067 --> 00:02:42,367
If the person diagnosing also sells the medicine, they might be tempted to prescribe more, and nobody double-checks their mistakes.

37
00:02:42,933 --> 00:02:51,533
Korea got there much later. Until 2000, it was common to get medicine straight from the clinic, or have a pharmacy make it up without a prescription.

38
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:57,767
In July 2000, the separation of prescribing and dispensing began: diagnosis and prescriptions at the clinic, medicine at the pharmacy.

39
00:02:57,833 --> 00:03:01,167
It was a rocky start, and opinions on it are still divided.

40
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:09,167
So does a pharmacist just pack what the prescription says? Before handing over the medicine, the pharmacist asked me something.

41
00:03:09,233 --> 00:03:13,267
"How much does your child weigh? Is he taking any other medicine these days?"

42
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:15,633
"Her weight is... uh..."

43
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:17,533
I got stuck on the weight.

44
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:26,267
Children's doses often depend on body weight. The pharmacist checks again that the prescription fits this child and doesn't clash with other medicine.

45
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:32,167
The law even says that if something about a prescription looks off, the pharmacist must check with the doctor before making it up.

46
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:35,467
Not someone who sells medicine. A second pair of eyes.

47
00:03:36,033 --> 00:03:40,433
While we waited, my kid pointed at the display rack by the register.

48
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:43,533
"Dad, you can just buy that medicine without the paper?"

49
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,200
This time, before answering, I looked at the pharmacist first.

50
00:03:47,767 --> 00:03:54,767
"That's an over-the-counter medicine, so you don't need a prescription. But you ask me before you buy it."

51
00:03:54,833 --> 00:04:00,233
Every medicine box says one of two things in small print: 'prescription drug' or 'over-the-counter drug'.

52
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:09,133
Prescription drugs need a doctor's exam and prescription. Over-the-counter drugs are the ones judged safe enough to use without seeing a doctor.

53
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:18,367
A few of those are even sold at 24-hour convenience stores for the night: fever and pain relievers, cold medicine, digestive aids, and pain patches.

54
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:23,000
They're called 'household safety medicines', and as of 2026 there are eleven of them.

55
00:04:23,567 --> 00:04:28,400
The second split is nursing. 1854, the Crimean War.

56
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:32,733
Florence Nightingale leads a group of nurses to a military hospital.

57
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:40,833
Doctors treat a patient and move on, but the patient lies there all night. Every night, Nightingale made her rounds with a lamp.

58
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:49,500
And she wrote everything down. With those records, she drew charts showing that far more soldiers died of disease and poor hygiene than of their wounds.

59
00:04:49,567 --> 00:04:54,467
Nightingale drew statistical charts. I didn't even know my own kid's weight.

60
00:04:54,533 --> 00:05:03,800
With a nursing school opening in London in 1860, nursing became a trained profession. In Korea, the title changed from 'ganhowon' to 'ganhosa' in 1987.

61
00:05:04,367 --> 00:05:14,100
A nurse's job isn't just helping the doctor. Watching the patient, keeping records, noticing when something's wrong and making a judgment: that's in the law too.

62
00:05:14,167 --> 00:05:19,700
And since 2025, there's a separate Nursing Act for nurses' licenses and work.

63
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:28,867
Nursing branches out, too. With experience, extra study and an exam, a nurse can become an advanced practice nurse in one field.

64
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:34,500
Anesthesia, emergency, intensive care, children, hospice... thirteen fields in all.

65
00:05:34,567 --> 00:05:39,100
They're the ones who stay by your side, even after the doctor has come and gone.

66
00:05:39,667 --> 00:05:41,000
So here it is.

67
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:42,800
Doctors decide what the illness is,

68
00:05:42,867 --> 00:05:44,467
pharmacists make up the medicine,

69
00:05:44,533 --> 00:05:46,300
and nurses stay by your side.

70
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:50,767
And it was split up on purpose, so that no one person does it all.

71
00:05:52,200 --> 00:06:00,700
That night, my kid's fever went up. I sat by the bed with a thermometer and gave the answer I couldn't give that afternoon.

72
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:09,900
"Remember I said the doctor's the boss? That was wrong. At the hospital, three people each have a different job."

73
00:06:09,967 --> 00:06:20,167
"The doctor figures out where it hurts and what illness it is. He looks at your throat, listens to your chest, and writes the medicine you need on a piece of paper."

74
00:06:20,233 --> 00:06:29,767
"The pharmacist takes that paper and makes the medicine. She checks again that it fits your weight and doesn't clash with other medicine."

75
00:06:29,833 --> 00:06:36,567
"The medicine on that rack is the kind you're allowed to buy without the paper. But you still ask the pharmacist first."

76
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:45,933
"The nurse stays with the sick person and keeps watching. She takes your temperature, writes it down, and is the first to notice if something's wrong."

77
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:53,700
"If one person did everything, no one would notice a mistake. So the three of them share the work and check each other."

78
00:06:54,267 --> 00:06:58,300
My kid looked at the thermometer in my hand and said,

79
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:02,533
"Then tonight, you're the nurse, Dad."

80
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,233
"Yeah. Just for tonight."

81
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,600
"Then don't forget to write it down."

82
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:13,833
That night, for the first time, I wrote down my kid's temperature with the time. 9:30 p.m., 38.1 degrees.

83
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:19,467
Could you tell me your kid's weight right now? I can, now.

84
00:07:19,533 --> 00:07:24,100
The next morning, fever gone, my kid looked at my note and asked another question.

85
00:07:24,167 --> 00:07:31,733
"Dad, the boy next to us at the clinic yesterday has the flu. What's the difference between a cold and the flu? Is the flu just a bad cold?"

86
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,700
"...It's time for your medicine. Medicine first."

87
00:07:34,267 --> 00:07:38,667
That's the next episode. I'll be off doing some more searching before then.
